goto = {import:goto-plugin}
remember = {import:remember-plugin}

location = darkroom // <-- default starting location
 
darkroom
	[location = "darkroom", ""]You're in a dark room. You can [goto(lightroom, "turn on the light")] or [goto(tavern, "exit through the door")].

lightroom
	[location = "lightroom", ""]You switch the light on. There are mops and buckets here. Seems like you're in a cleaning cupboard. You can [goto(darkroom, "turn off the light")] or [goto(tavern, "exit through the door")].

tavern
	[location = "tavern", ""]You open the door and walk out. You're standing in an old dusty tavern. There's no one here. You can [goto(tavernexit, "look for an exit")] or [goto(tavernbar, "have a look at the bar")].

tavernexit
	[location = "tavernexit", ""]etc... (tavernexit)

tavernbar
	[location = "tavernbar", ""]etc... (tavernbar)
	
	
// Notice in the HTML panel I've written this: [root[location]]
// `root` is a special variable that refers to the "root" of your perchance tree.
// In other words, it's the list that contains all your lists!
// So in this case it's a perchance list with the folloring properties/items:
//    goto
//    remember
//    location
//    darkroom
//    lightroom
//    tavern
//    tavernexit
//    tavernbar
// So we could write [root.tavernbar] and it would actually be the same as writing [tavernbar].
// The reason it's useful here is because the list that we want to output first in this generator
// is not "fixed". It depends on the text that is stored in the `location` variable. So we need to use
// "dynamic sublist/subproperty referencing" which is explained on the perchance.org/tutorial page
// so that we can grab the sublist of root which has the same name as the text that is stored in the
// `location` variable. If this is confusing, just read the dynamic referencing stuff on the perchance.org/tutorial
// page and that should clear things up.
// The main thing to understand here is that [root[location]] just outputs the list (e.g. darkroom, lightroom, etc.) which
// has the same name as the text that is stored in the `location` variable.